---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CalcMoneyDebtPayoff from '@/components/tools/finance/CalcMoneyDebtPayoff.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="debt-payoff-calculator">
  <CalcMoneyDebtPayoff client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Two orders, one engine</h2>
    <p>
      Snowball and avalanche use the same budget in a different order. In both, you pay the minimum on
      each debt and put all remaining money on one target. When that target is cleared, its minimum
      joins the extra money and the whole payment moves to the next debt. The only difference is which
      debt goes first.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Avalanche</strong> targets the highest APR first. It always costs the least interest,
        because it clears the most expensive debt first.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Snowball</strong> targets the smallest balance first. It costs more, sometimes only
        slightly, but it clears a debt sooner, and a cleared debt helps people continue.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The comparison above runs both methods on your debts and reports the exact difference. That
      difference is the cost of the early win, and it is the honest way to choose.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the rollover matters more than the order</h2>
    <p>
      The order changes the total by a few per cent. The rollover changes it by an order of magnitude.
      For example, three debts with minimums of 35, 110 and 265 and an extra 200 a month:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[420px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Stage</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Payment at the target debt</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="tabular-nums">
          <tr><td>All three open</td><td style="text-align:right">200 + its own minimum</td></tr>
          <tr><td>After the first clears</td><td style="text-align:right">200 + 35 + its own minimum</td></tr>
          <tr><td>After the second clears</td><td style="text-align:right">200 + 35 + 110 + its own minimum</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      At the last debt, the whole monthly budget of 610 goes to one balance. For this reason payoff
      plans get much faster towards the end. If you stop halfway and spend a freed-up minimum, you lose
      most of the benefit.
    </p>

    <h2>Fixing the order, and why</h2>
    <p>
      The debt payoff calculator sets the payoff order once, from today's balances and rates, and keeps it
      for the whole plan. Sorting again each month as balances change gives a slightly different total
      and a plan that is hard to follow. A debt plan depends on behaviour as much as on finance: if it
      does not fit on an index card, you will probably stop by month four.
    </p>

    <h2>What to include, and what to leave out</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Include credit cards, store cards, overdrafts, personal loans, car finance, buy-now-pay-later
        balances and all debts at a rate above what savings earn.
      </li>
      <li>
        Leave out a low-rate mortgage. Under avalanche it sorts last and stays there for decades,
        filling the schedule with no useful information. Model it separately with an amortization
        schedule.
      </li>
      <li>
        Think carefully about student loans. Income-contingent systems (UK Plan 2, US IDR plans) work
        very differently from a fixed instalment loan, and overpaying can be wasted if the balance will
        be written off.
      </li>
      <li>
        Build a small emergency buffer first. If you clear a card and borrow on it again after a car
        repair, you lose money. One month of expenses in cash before the plan starts is usually correct.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Reading the month-by-month plan</h2>
    <p>
      Print the plan table. Each row is one month: the total paid, the interest part, the debt removed,
      and what remains. The milestone column marks the months when a debt clears. Circle those rows;
      they keep you motivated.
    </p>

    <h2>Where the numbers come from</h2>
    <p>
      Each month, the simulation charges interest on each open balance at its own monthly rate
      (APR ÷ 12), pays the stated minimum on each debt except the target, then puts the rest of the
      budget on the target. Balances are arbitrary-precision decimals, rounded to the minor unit at each
      interest charge, as on a card statement. If the budget cannot exceed the combined interest, the
      calculator says so and does not show a fifty-year plan.
    </p>

    <h2>Nothing leaves this tab, and nothing is stored by default</h2>
    <p>
      There is no server behind this page and no analytics. Saving is off unless you turn it on. When it
      is on, it writes to the local storage of this browser on this device only, and the delete button
      next to the switch removes the data immediately. You can work through the whole plan with the
      network disconnected.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
